### What is Collectors.teeing?

Collectors.teeing (Java 12) runs two collectors on the same stream simultaneously and merges their results.

### How do you compute min and max in one pass?

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.Optional;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

record SalaryRange(Optional<BigDecimal> min, Optional<BigDecimal> max) {}

public class SalaryAnalytics {

    public SalaryRange getRange(java.util.List<BigDecimal> salaries) {
        return salaries.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.teeing(
                Collectors.minBy(Comparator.naturalOrder()),  // downstream 1
                Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.naturalOrder()),  // downstream 2
                SalaryRange::new));                            // merger
    }
}
```

```text
Input: [900000, 1200000, 1500000, 1800000]
Result: SalaryRange{min=Optional[900000], max=Optional[1800000]}
One pass through the data.
```

### Why is one pass better than two?

```java
// WRONG: two passes over the same data
Optional<BigDecimal> min = salaries.stream().min(Comparator.naturalOrder());
Optional<BigDecimal> max = salaries.stream().max(Comparator.naturalOrder());
// Two iterations!

// CORRECT: one pass with teeing
var range = salaries.stream().collect(Collectors.teeing(
    Collectors.minBy(Comparator.naturalOrder()),
    Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.naturalOrder()),
    SalaryRange::new));
// One iteration!
```

### How do you compute count and sum together?

```java
record CountAndSum(long count, BigDecimal sum) {
    BigDecimal average() {
        return count == 0 ? BigDecimal.ZERO : sum.divide(
            BigDecimal.valueOf(count), 2, java.math.RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }
}

CountAndSum result = trades.stream()
    .map(Trade::getAmount)
    .collect(Collectors.teeing(
        Collectors.counting(),
        Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add),
        CountAndSum::new));
// CountAndSum{count=6, sum=1185737}
// result.average() → 197622.83
```

### How do you use teeing with filtering?

```java
// Separate settled and unsettled totals in one pass
record SettlementSummary(BigDecimal settledTotal, BigDecimal unsettledTotal) {}

SettlementSummary summary = trades.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.teeing(
        Collectors.filtering(Trade::isSettled,
            Collectors.mapping(Trade::getAmount,
                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add))),
        Collectors.filtering(t -> !t.isSettled(),
            Collectors.mapping(Trade::getAmount,
                Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add))),
        SettlementSummary::new));
```

### Can you nest teeing for more than two results?

```java
// Four metrics in one pass: min, max, count, sum
record FullStats(SalaryRange range, CountAndSum countSum) {}

FullStats stats = amounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.teeing(
        Collectors.teeing(                                    // inner teeing 1
            Collectors.minBy(Comparator.naturalOrder()),
            Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.naturalOrder()),
            SalaryRange::new),
        Collectors.teeing(                                    // inner teeing 2
            Collectors.counting(),
            Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add),
            CountAndSum::new),
        FullStats::new));                                      // outer merger
// All four metrics computed in a single pass!
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: merger returns wrong type
var result = stream.collect(Collectors.teeing(
    Collectors.counting(),
    Collectors.summingInt(Trade::getQuantity),
    (count, sum) -> count));  // discards sum! Why use teeing then?

// CORRECT: use both results
var result = stream.collect(Collectors.teeing(
    Collectors.counting(),
    Collectors.summingInt(Trade::getQuantity),
    (count, sum) -> new CountAndQuantity(count, sum)));
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- For empty streams, both downstream collectors return their identity values (Optional.empty for minBy/maxBy, 0 for counting).
- teeing is especially powerful as a downstream inside groupingBy: per-group min+max in one pass.
- The merger BiFunction can be a record constructor (cleanest), a lambda, or any BiFunction.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you have more than two collectors? (Not directly. Nest teeing calls or use a custom collector.)
- Does teeing work with parallel streams? (Yes. Both downstream collectors handle parallel splitting independently.)
- What if one downstream returns Optional.empty? (The merger receives Optional.empty; handle it there.)
